Evnia Precision Center for Linux
================================

This is an unofficial, offline Linux port of Evnia Precision Center 1.13.0,
limited to the monitor: picture modes (SmartImage), game settings, input,
audio, system settings, profiles, the dashboard overlay and Ambiglow
lighting. Philips peripherals (mice, keyboards, headsets, lamps), firmware
updates, accounts and every other online feature are removed. The program
makes no network connection; a kill-switch blocks any request that is not
local.

Start it from the application menu ("Evnia Precision Center") or with the
command evnia-precision-center. See evnia-precision-center(1).


Reset and Factory reset also reset the monitor
----------------------------------------------

Like the Windows application, two functions send the monitor the DDC/CI
command "Restore factory defaults" (VCP 0x04 = 1):

  * Profile -> Reset (reset the current profile)
  * Settings -> Factory reset

Every OSD picture setting of the monitor (SmartImage mode values,
brightness, contrast, colour, the monitor's own Ambiglow settings, ...)
returns to its factory value. The program waits 5 seconds and then reads
the monitor again. Factory reset additionally deletes the program's themes,
profiles, macros and settings in ~/.config/EvniaServe (the logs folder is
kept) and recreates the defaults. Both operations take about 6 to 10
seconds; the monitor may blank briefly.


How the monitor is reached, and permissions
-------------------------------------------

The program talks to the monitor over the USB-C/USB upstream cable (the
monitor's built-in hub has a USB-DDC bridge, VIA Labs 2109:8884, and an
Ambiglow controller, ENE 0cf2:a201). Without the USB cable it falls back to
DDC/CI over the graphics card's i2c buses (/dev/i2c-N).

The package installs what the logged-in user needs for this; no group
membership and no root access are required:

  /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-evnia-precision-center.rules
      TAG+="uaccess" (access for the user at the local seat) for exactly
      2109:8884 and 0cf2:a201, and for the i2c buses of display adapters
      (not for the graphics card's SMBus and "AMDGPU SMU" buses, which
      stay root-only). The USB-DDC bridge is also kept out of USB autosuspend.
  /usr/lib/modules-load.d/evnia-precision-center-i2c.conf
      loads the i2c-dev module at boot (the package loads it once at
      installation too).

The rules apply to devices that are present at installation. If the
monitor is not found, re-plug the monitor's USB upstream cable, or log out
and in again. To check the access rights:

  getfacl /dev/bus/usb/$(lsusb -d 2109:8884 | awk '{print $2 "/" substr($4, 1, 3)}')

When the Ambiglow controller cannot be opened, the program uses the
monitor's own Ambiglow over DDC/CI instead and writes a warning to its log.


Desktop notes
-------------

  * Wayland: "Follow video" Ambiglow asks for screen-sharing permission
    (the desktop portal dialog) once per program run, and GNOME shows its
    screen-sharing indicator while it runs.
  * Ambiglow "Follow video" has a Speed slider (with the USB Ambiglow
    controller): Low is the Windows app's timing, Normal (default) follows
    the screen faster, High more smoothly and with more CPU. Changing it
    keeps the running screen capture (no new portal dialog).
  * It also has a Brightness slider (Bright, Brighter, Brightest = the
    Windows app's full colours) and an opt-in "Fast LED upload
    (experimental)" checkbox: one USB transfer per LED update instead of
    six. The checkbox is untested on real monitors; untick it if the LEDs
    flicker or freeze (the setting is kept in ~/.config/evnia/config.json
    as "linuxExperimental").
  * Wayland: themes bound to an application do not switch automatically;
    the active window is only known on X11.
  * The tray icon needs a StatusNotifier host. On GNOME that is the
    "AppIndicator and KStatusNotifierItem Support" extension (package
    gnome-shell-extension-appindicator). Without a tray, closing the window
    quits the program, and a start "minimized to tray" shows the window.
  * "Follow audio" records the default output's monitor with parec
    (package pulseaudio-utils; PulseAudio or PipeWire with pipewire-pulse).
    GNOME may show its microphone indicator while it runs.
  * "Start with the system" writes ~/.config/autostart/
    evnia-precision-center.desktop. It is off by default.


Files
-----

  ~/.config/evnia/            program settings (config.json) and logs
                              (logs/YY-MM-DD.log)
  ~/.config/EvniaServe/       themes, profiles, macros, the monitor
                              capability cache and the backend logs
                              (logs/YYYY-MM-DD.txt)

Settings and profiles of the Windows application can be taken over: before
the first start, copy the folders %APPDATA%\evnia and %APPDATA%\EvniaServe
of the Windows installation to ~/.config/evnia and ~/.config/EvniaServe.
Profiles exported from the Windows application (.pcenter files) can also be
imported in Profile.

Debug logging: create the file "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/evnia-debug-open.tmp"
(touch it) and start the program. The log level is then debug and
Alt+Shift+M opens the developer tools. The file is removed when the program
exits.


Removal
-------

"apt purge evnia-precision-center" removes every file of the package. It
does not touch the files in the users' home directories: remove
~/.config/evnia, ~/.config/EvniaServe and, if present,
~/.config/autostart/evnia-precision-center.desktop yourself.


Sandbox
-------

The Chromium renderer runs sandboxed. Where unprivileged user namespaces
are not available (for example on Ubuntu 24.04, whose AppArmor policy
restricts them), Chromium uses its setuid helper
/opt/evnia-precision-center/chrome-sandbox, which the package installs
root-owned with mode 4755.
